{{Politics of Balisca}}

A '''confederal institution''' ({{Langx|bl|institución confederal}}) is a legal and political entity within the [[Balisca|Baliscan Confederation]] that exercises authority delegated to it by the [[Regions of Balisca|autonomous regions]].

Unlike government institutions in [[W:Federalism|federal systems]] (such as the [[United States]] or [[Germany]]), Baliscan confederal institutions do not possess inherent or supreme sovereignty. Instead, they operate on the principle of inverted sovereignty (alternatively known as radical subsidiarity), meaning they derive their legitimacy solely from the voluntary association of the lower political divisions. Confederal institutions exist to manage "Common Affairs" (''asuntos común'')—such as national defense, international diplomacy, epidemiological control, and [[Essential industry|strategic industrial planning]]—that cannot be effectively handled at the [[Communes of Balisca|communal]], [[Cantons of Balisca|cantonal]] or [[Regions of Balisca|regional]] level.

Confederal institutions are characterized by their lack of coercive police power over the the lower political levels, relying instead on [[W:Arbitration|arbitration]] while striving for (but not always achieving) [[W:Consensus|consensus]].

== Constitutional basis ==
The legal framework for confederal institutions is established in [[Confederal Charter]] of the [[Constitution of Balisca|Baliscan Constitution]]. The Constitution defines these bodies not as rulers of the population, but as "administrative coordinators of the collective will." The guiding principle of the Baliscan political system is the concept of the "[[W:Minarchy|servant state]]".

Baliscan political theory dictates that power flows upwards:

# '''The people''' exercise their power/will at the '''[[Communes of Balisca|commune]]'''.
# '''The commune''' grants power to the '''[[Cantons of Balisca|canton]]'''.
# '''The canton''' grants power to the '''[[Regions of Balisca|region]]'''.
# '''The regions''' grant specific, limited competencies to the '''[[Confederal government of Balisca|confederal government]]'''.

Any power not explicitly granted to a confederal institution remains with the regions. Furthermore, the personnel staffing the political institutions (such as the [[Confederal Congress]]) are often subject to the [[W:Imperative mandate|imperative mandate]]—they must vote according to the specific instructions of their home region, rather than their own conscience, with the goal of preventing the formation of a detached federal political class.

== Types of institutions ==
Confederal institutions are generally categorized into three branches based on their function and governance structure:

=== I. Political institutions ===
These bodies facilitate decision-making and legislation regarding the Confederation as a whole.

* '''The [[Confederal Congress]]:''' The primary legislative body. It is a unicameral parliament composed of delegates from the 45 regions. It sets the Confederal budget and ratifies international treaties.
* '''The [[Secretariat Council (Balisca)|Secretariat Council]]:''' The executive committee responsible for the day-to-day administration of the government. It is collegial, meaning there is no single "President" with executive authority.

=== II. Arbitral and judicial institutions ===
Because the Confederation lacks the power to unilaterally impose laws on regions, it relies heavily on mediation.

* '''The [[Confederal Council of Arbitration]]:''' The supreme dispute resolution body. It mediates conflicts between regions or between a region and the confederal administration. Its authority relies on the voluntary consent of the involved.

=== III. Administrative and industrial institutions ===
This is the largest and most powerful branch of the confederal level, comprising the bureaucracy that manages the "common affairs" of the confederation.

* '''[[Secretariat Council (Balisca)|Confederal secretariats]]:''' Technocratic bodies that enforce national standards in specific sectors, such as the '''[[Secretariat of Health and Care Services (Balisca)|Secretariat of Health and Care Services]] (SECSSA)''' or the [[Secretariat of Education and Integration (Balisca)|'''Secretariat of Education and Integration''']] '''(SECEI)'''.
* '''[[Essential industry|Essential industries]] ([[W:State owned enterprise|SOEs]]):''' Strategic economic entities fully owned by the confederal government to ensure national sovereignty. These include [[Baliscan Semiconductor Corporation|BSC]] (semiconductors), [[Energía Común]] (national grid), [[Astilleros Baliscanos]] (naval shipyards) and [[Auster]] (aerospace design/manufacturing). These entities are unique because, unlike political bodies, they operate with significant autonomy to compete in the global market.

== Powers and competencies ==
While limited, the powers of confederal institutions are absolute within their specific domains. This is often described as "narrow but deep" authority.

* '''Strategic planning:''' The Confederation has the sole authority to plan critical infrastructure that spans the confederal territory (i.e. the national energy grid and [[AVB|high-speed rail network]]).
* '''International relations:''' Only the Confederal Congress [[Confederal emergency|may declare war]], sign peace treaties, or accredit ambassadors.
* '''Economic standardization:''' The Confederation sets the "ecological ceiling" standards and the [[Equitable Society Compact]] parameters (such as the 5:1 income ratio) that all cooperatives must follow.
* '''Health emergencies:''' As seen in the SECSSA, the Confederation can assume temporary direct control of regional health assets during declared pandemics (such as the [[COVID-19 pandemic in Balisca]]).

== Criticism and tension ==
The nature of confederal institutions is a subject of constant political debate in Balisca.

